-8bf570a72393" :topic/math-academy-id 849} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32977988-9ddf-5f51-8216-474ed5b18590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372491}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372491} :answer-field/id #uuid "f56c2aba-5529-57e1-a79a-70813d89b492" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372490} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1123100d-4da4-58aa-b750-ca1b46eba376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372493}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372493} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4abeb6-cc64-59bf-97b7-a7eab1dbd17f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "51372c7c-b0b0-5674-a112-93ab7dec687c" :question/math-academy-id "q-278317" :question/problem "The center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9}=1$ is located at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=4$ and $k=-4,$ so the center is located at $(\\,4,-4\\,).$"} :step/id #uuid "036e2b13-48e5-5f1c-9ff0-5814ddca4cfd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b57d90-a9ee-54f9-840f-6dfdd3aab51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416677} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3d7e26-2073-5af5-a3f5-d42d26f20154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416679} {:answer/id #uuid "4376677f-9a7f-532f-b66b-ad4902b9c630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416680} {:answer/id #uuid "b2919188-309d-5e3e-965d-f1e83d98bddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416681} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e0ee02-13c6-544f-b4b9-39031730f3ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416682} :answer-field/id #uuid "759f0728-d8d2-515a-b991-0560a3f812fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ee934c99-4f27-5203-a0f5-c13e99354b00" :question/math-academy-id "q-7630" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2cabfed6c7008b47c3157cca4862d0c2a5b2a2b34e1bb35aeea963b1639af8c.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=1,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "af068651-d4d2-5cf0-9c59-16a00c0246fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416690}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23962a06-bb97-5fbd-a080-34dac0fedadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416678} :answer-field/id #uuid "43d267b9-ec22-5b8b-a0c1-af357d98c1d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d94a5264-18c5-5679-b580-fde7816b3b57" :question/math-academy-id "q-278253" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a8e0f0c766d2674dbf32feac760baea28418d65565fd6d64dccc6cf3eecd6bf.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-3,4),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-3))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bfc51aac-6584-5b70-b332-8fd371dd660d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a0e5e1-b1da-5dc9-b41e-3bb393e24954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247432} {:answer/id #uuid "35585ca0-a98f-5baf-94fe-509a8b5d8a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247903} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc151f9-95d7-5299-ad1e-76f145c3c6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248525} {:answer/id #uuid "82cb959f-6960-532f-bba7-b7925ec3c118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248581} {:answer/id #uuid "be580972-e9e1-565a-a446-3b2e2bb49a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249116}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249116} :answer-field/id #uuid "164d9e4d-3f7a-5463-987e-751c5fbca993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e17dda64-b9d6-527b-acc5-6bdc5ef17fe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115297" :question/problem "![](images/10/1006eabe3321beb48df2cd936ff2252b77e8ded81f9c5b7aec4d143e43941088.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-4,0),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f12ab8ea-ca60-5bc7-a665-f8bdd2c2c872" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416689}}] :activity/title "Equations of Ellipses Centered at a General Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416676}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436953} :activity/id #uuid "81f168ca-75ef-55be-93ec-76dca916c2c6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699 :topic/id #uuid "0c8c634b-da7d-56b9-a349-8bf570a72393" :topic/math-academy-id 849} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8434a84-5ef1-505b-a2c7-d42809c26959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401560} {:answer/id #uuid "386a0708-44f8-5648-9834-907d2d7fef7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401561} {:answer/id #uuid "f019f459-9c8d-5bca-ac2e-8bf08a5f03f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401562} {:answer/id #uuid "1362416b-6b2e-5b41-8cce-b430bb96c908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401563} {:answer/id #uuid "c489543f-9ba5-563a-8700-e139b5d4d02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401563} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fd9c31-b922-5bc4-b3d4-983574117b6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8cb21ae0-f2f0-5250-97c3-5094275c61e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-16465" :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{20}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-1$ and $k=-3,$ so the center is located at $(-1,-3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=5$ and ${b}^{2}=20.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=5,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=20,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c2676447-f702-5622-a1e3-b995f97745d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436955}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294897 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d981ebdf-12c2-56c7-b5f4-eeb735bd889e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293775} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6e60596-98ff-5ec0-8a1b-9ea8b40d91ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294530} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4109d59a-1fba-553c-aee8-88bf1d81cdb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293196} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc4abb2c-00c8-5fa2-9a85-dde3a949863f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "69b56d75-ccf1-51bc-b43e-408c64b94822" :question/math-academy-id "q-277200" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-4$ and $k=-3,$ so the center is located at $(-4,-3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=36$ and ${b}^{2}=12.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=36,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=12,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "d6715709-7fa0-5c6a-bd95-a26942b6d206"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436955 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d360012-b71b-5e3d-a5ed-050551fedc08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336156} {:answer/id #uuid "363d6738-eccc-55d0-b37d-dc6eeb5492c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336536} {:answer/id #uuid "799fad89-4775-5c00-9f34-a99bb77a3ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337125} {:answer/id #uuid "975b13c5-78a1-50ac-8fca-df15265468b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337411} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e5af31-ccd8-5042-a136-b3b71e9698e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337411} :answer-field/id #uuid "e195d8c3-ee86-56e6-9272-e56918978464" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "82c10773-d500-51bb-9b6a-76b40d3c3844" :question/math-academy-id "q-72252" :question/problem "![](images/28/28079ead5ebb4649900653172d11c25f3ff489c6b34772094a1ba947c18ef3e1.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-2,-2),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f8bb185e-5399-55d1-ad1f-3a1efd9c660a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436954}}] :activity/title "Equations of Ellipses Centered at a General Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436952}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442287} :activity/id #uuid "3feb4992-432e-5c94-9938-9677fea30f81" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699 :topic/id #uuid "0c8c634b-da7d-56b9-a349-8bf570a72393" :topic/math-academy-id 849} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442284 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a7b612-819f-5a1c-a8b0-8ec256d1a50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293006}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293006} :answer-field/id #uuid "6104b68e-20e5-53b0-9017-463931726fac" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294275} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e075ac1-0b6f-5a1b-9f7a-153a820b245a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293313} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0814c7b-ca42-54f0-a23e-73619c3fcf74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a4cc48c-e200-5367-abd6-f0488138a7ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-278404" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{81}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=3$ and $k=-8,$ so the center is located at $(3,-8).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=8$ and ${b}^{2}=81.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=8,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=81,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{81}=9.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=9,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 9=18,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "479cce25-13e7-5cc9-8b50-cb0c7ebd0554"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442285 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d35968d-5c44-5e65-a197-520a366d9bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442279}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442279} :answer-field/id #uuid "344c48c4-4333-53a2-91c2-910da2275099" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442281} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6350dda7-0009-5d3a-9b8a-8e8d61699c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442280} :answer-field/id #uuid "72c3b159-addc-58ed-8523-aec1a6a17d47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "35dee2e4-b90f-575e-8987-9a600b4e60ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-278400" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=-1,$ so the center is located at $(2,-1).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=18$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=18,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=3\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}=6\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8.$"} :step/id #uuid "5e449fb1-4c6a-52d2-ba8a-4d3471a77fb8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442286}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442286 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a81632cc-b5b8-5293-854a-15d0e07860c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293597}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293597} :answer-field/id #uuid "17f02c76-60a4-5afe-a558-bf595f597fe6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fee5cb0e-afb7-5315-bc6f-231125cf2b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293929}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293929} :answer-field/id #uuid "95c691bd-bcc5-5404-b7f6-e6dcdb4b61e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f193e9d2-f5fe-5d88-ae37-9891fd4ddd44" :question/math-academy-id "q-278403" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{15}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{49}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=1$ and $k=6,$ so the center is located at $(1,6).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=15$ and ${b}^{2}=49.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=15,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{15}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=49,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{15}=2\\sqrt{15}.$"} :step/id #uuid "5f6429ed-2ed5-5a9e-8dc2-d2bd00c04dcd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442284}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8866d4a7-b007-5b5c-8db0-e149a146f1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293463} :answer-field/id #uuid "08e66c54-d1b0-5a37-9793-f7b1b263205c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c287975d-2210-588b-808c-37d4aa02c574" :question/math-academy-id "q-278395" :question/problem "![](images/33/33adaa159f0926038c6b04a750b6a61b8a18e0fe2df9c6c3c581c139e27b2c95.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(6,0),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ea8cfec-9867-5979-9717-6e4a67bd667e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442288}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea709181-3435-5cda-a381-1ffb34dc4957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293847} :answer-field/id #uuid "b39106f6-0d4a-50b5-a684-3a9dd19496c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58d53b95-48c4-5460-b91e-ffee8730045f" :question/math-academy-id "q-278398" :question/problem "![](images/44/441ee336bc10f5787a5956a6c118cde9faaa0485c3c2f8a6adb9dd93903b3972.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(0,5),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f7f3c721-aa49-5677-9ac6-7f79cac99137" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442285}}] :activity/title "Equations of Ellipses Centered at a General Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442278}]}
